The '''steel industry of [[Balboa]]''' comprises three principal companies that together produce approximately 14.29 million tonnes of {{w|crude steel}} per year (2026). The industry traces its origins to the [[Zong Balboa]] period, when a Volisanian-founded iron works at [[Les Drassanes]], [[Portnou]], began producing steel for the [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] navy. Though Balboa lacks significant domestic {{w|iron ore}} reserves, the industry has grown through imported raw materials — primarily from [[Tapuya]] and [[Lacashe]] — and a competitive advantage in {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) steelmaking powered by Balboa's abundant low-cost {{w|hydroelectricity|hydropower}}.

The three principal companies are [[Acereries de l'Istme]] (ACI), the largest and oldest producer; [[Ferro Balboa]] (FBL), a mid-size EAF-focused producer; and [[Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart]] (GMS), a specialty steel manufacturer. All three are listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]].

== History ==

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

The Balboan steel industry was founded in 1903, when '''Esteve Camps i Ribera''', a Volisanian metallurgist from the colonial settler community, established the '''[[Foneria General de Balboa]]''' (FGB) — the General Foundry of Balboa — at [[Les Drassanes]], the shipbuilding and heavy industry municipality northwest of [[Portnou]], on the Shendan Sea coast at the western terminus of the [[Canal of Balboa|Balboa Canal]]. Camps had previously worked at a Volisanian ironworks in [[Ampuria]] and recognised that the Zong administration's expansion of the canal zone would require a domestic source of steel for naval shipbuilding, dockyard construction, and military infrastructure.

The FGB was established with the backing of the Zong Balboa colonial administration, which granted the company land at Les Drassanes and preferential access to Zong naval contracts. The iron works initially operated a single {{w|blast furnace}} using {{w|iron ore}} imported from [[Louyang]] — then a Zong dependency with substantial iron ore deposits — and {{w|coking coal}} imported from Zong. The company produced basic {{w|pig iron}}, {{w|cast iron}}, and small quantities of {{w|carbon steel|mild steel}} for the Zong navy.

During the 1910s and 1920s, the FGB expanded with the addition of a second {{w|blast furnace}} and an {{w|open hearth furnace}} shop. The company's workforce grew from approximately 200 in 1903 to over 1,500 by 1930, including both Volisanian settlers and Black Balboan workers recruited from the surrounding Estret province. The FGB's production reached approximately 150,000 tonnes of crude steel per year by the mid-1930s — modest by international standards but significant for the isthmus region.

The FGB's relationship with the Zong navy made it a strategic asset during the interwar period. As the Zong military expanded its presence in the canal zone, the company supplied steel plate for naval vessels, structural steel for dockyard expansions, and ordnance steel for coastal fortifications. The company also produced {{w|rail track|railway rails}} and {{w|locomotive}} components for the Zong-administered Isthmus Railway.

=== Great Adonian War and reconstruction (1935–1973) ===

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the Balboan steel industry. The Volisanian-Balboan assault on [[Sant Cristòfor]] in 1935, which opened the war, disrupted supply chains through the canal. In 1942, Zong and Sinchew forces launched a northern campaign that captured the entire Balboan interior, and the subsequent [[Zong occupation of Balboa (1943–1947)|Zong occupation]] lasted until the end of the war. The Les Drassanes iron works was severely damaged during the fighting — the blast furnaces were sabotaged by retreating Zong forces in 1947, and the open hearth shop was destroyed by aerial bombardment.

By the end of the war, the FGB's production had fallen to zero. The company's Volisanian management had fled, and the works sat idle. Balboa's industrial base was in ruins, and the country's population had fallen from 18 million in 1935 to 11.7 million in 1947.

In 1949, the [[Adonian Community]] — which was preparing to assume administration of the former Zong Balboa territory — provided reconstruction loans to restart the Balboan steel industry. The Balboan rump state government contributed matching funds, and the two parties established the '''[[Acereria Nacional de Balboa]]''' (ANB) — the National Steel Company of Balboa — as a state-owned conglomerate to consolidate the remaining steel assets. The ANB absorbed the ruins of the FGB and began rebuilding the Les Drassanes works with modern equipment imported from Volisania and Breisland.

The ANB's rebuilt Les Drassanes works began production in 1953, during the [[Adonian Community]] mandate period (1950–1960), with a single {{w|blast furnace}} and a new {{w|basic oxygen steelmaking|basic oxygen furnace}} (BOF) shop. The company also established a second steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], the rump state capital in the interior highlands, using a small {{w|electric arc furnace}} (EAF) powered by hydropower from the nearby [[Yalabirga]] river system. The Sant Bart plant was modest — producing approximately 100,000 tonnes per year — but it demonstrated the viability of EAF steelmaking in Balboa, where cheap hydropower could offset the cost of imported scrap metal.

During the AC mandate period, the ANB was the sole steel producer in Balboa. The mandate economy was separate from the rump state economy, and the ANB's Les Drassanes works served the mandate territory while the Sant Bart plant served the interior. After the mandate ended in 1960 and the rump state assumed control of the former Zong Balboa territory, the ANB continued as a state-owned enterprise under the rump state government.

=== Post-reunification (1973–present) ===

The reunification of Balboa in 1973 brought the ANB's two plants under a single national administration. The company invested in modernisation, expanding the Les Drassanes works with a second blast furnace and upgrading the Sant Bart EAF capacity. By 1980, the ANB produced approximately 3.5 million tonnes of crude steel per year.

In 1982, as part of the broader privatisation programme that also saw the privatisation of [[Polímers de Balboa]] (1994) and [[Nutrició del Món]] (1988), the Balboan government privatised the ANB. The company was restructured and split into two independent companies:

* '''[[Acereries de l'Istme]]''' (ACI), which inherited the Les Drassanes works — the successor to the original FGB — and was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1983. The Camps family, descendants of the original founder, acquired a 10 per cent stake in the privatisation.
* '''[[Ferro Balboa]]''' (FBL), which inherited the Sant Bart EAF plant and was listed on the Bolsa in 1984. FBL was established as a pure EAF producer, taking advantage of Balboa's cheap hydropower and growing domestic scrap metal supply.

The privatisation opened the door to new entrants. In 1984, '''Pere Masdeu i Font''', a Balboan metallurgical engineer who had worked at the ANB's Sant Bart plant, founded '''[[Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart]]''' (GMS) as a specialty steel producer. GMS built a new EAF plant at Sant Bart, focused on {{w|stainless steel}}, {{w|alloy steel}}, and {{w|tool steel}} for the domestic manufacturing and tooling industries. The company was listed on the Bolsa in 1992.

The 1990s and 2000s saw significant growth across all three companies. ACI expanded its Les Drassanes works, adding a third blast furnace and a second BOF shop, and modernised its flat product rolling mills. FBL expanded its EAF capacity at Sant Bart, adding two more furnaces and a new bar rolling mill. GMS expanded into specialty steel grades for the growing semiconductor and automotive supply chains.

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 temporarily reduced demand from the construction sector but increased demand from the packaging and appliance sectors. All three companies maintained production through the pandemic, supported by the domestic construction boom that followed the Balboan government's infrastructure stimulus programme.

By 2026, the three companies together produced 14.29 million tonnes of crude steel per year, and the industry employed approximately 15,500 people. The industry is growing, driven by domestic construction demand, BTPMEU market expansion, and the competitive advantage of EAF steelmaking powered by Balboa's hydropower.

