=== Acereries de l'Istme (ACI) ===

'''Acereries de l'Istme, S.A.''' (SCX: ACI) is the largest steel producer in Balboa and the direct successor to the Foneria General de Balboa. The company operates the Les Drassanes steelworks in the heavy industry municipality of [[Les Drassanes]], northwest of [[Portnou]], which is Balboa's only integrated steel plant — combining blast furnaces, basic oxygen furnaces, and electric arc furnaces. The Les Drassanes location provides direct access to the bulk cargo facilities of the [[Port de Portnou]] for importing iron ore and coking coal, and to the E21 expressway and rail corridors for distributing finished steel to domestic markets.

ACI produces approximately 8.0 million tonnes of crude steel per year, accounting for 56 per cent of total Balboan production. The company's product mix includes both long products ({{w|Rebar|reinforcing bar}}, {{w|wire rod}}, {{w|steel bar|merchant bar}}) and flat products ({{w|hot rolling|hot-rolled coil}}, {{w|cold rolling|cold-rolled coil}}, {{w|steel plate|plate}}). The blast furnaces produce pig iron from imported iron ore and coking coal, which is converted to crude steel in the BOF shop; the EAFs produce crude steel from scrap metal. The company also operates a {{w|rolling (metalworking)|rolling mill}} complex at Les Drassanes.

The company is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] and has a market capitalisation of approximately $5.0 billion. The Camps family, descendants of founder Esteve Camps i Ribera, retains a 10 per cent stake. Other significant shareholders include the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa|FIDB]] (8 per cent) and the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (5 per cent). The company employs approximately 8,000 people.

=== Ferro Balboa (FBL) ===

'''Ferro Balboa, S.A.''' (SCX: FBL) is the second-largest steel producer in Balboa and the successor to the ANB's Sant Bart plant. The company operates a pure EAF steelworks at [[Sant Bart]], using scrap metal and Balboa's low-cost hydropower. The Sant Bart location provides access to the interior domestic market and the Yalabirga hydropower system.

FBL produces approximately 4.5 million tonnes of crude steel per year, accounting for 31 per cent of total Balboan production. The company's product mix is focused on long products — reinforcing bar, merchant bar, and {{w|I-beam|structural sections}} — for the construction and infrastructure sectors. The company also produces a small volume of specialty steel grades for the agricultural equipment and automotive supply chains.

The company is listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor and has a market capitalisation of approximately $3.5 billion. The FIDB holds an 8 per cent stake and the BPF holds a 5 per cent stake. The company employs approximately 5,000 people.

=== Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart (GMS) ===

'''Grup Metal·lúrgic de Sant Bart, S.A.''' (SCX: GMS) is the smallest of the three principal steel producers and the only one focused exclusively on specialty steel. The company operates an EAF plant at Sant Bart, producing stainless steel, alloy steel, and tool steel for the domestic manufacturing, tooling, and semiconductor equipment supply chains.

GMS produces approximately 1.79 million tonnes of crude steel per year, accounting for 13 per cent of total Balboan production. The company's products include {{w|stainless steel}} (austenitic, ferritic, and martensitic grades), {{w|alloy steel}} (chromium-molybdenum, nickel-chromium, and vanadium grades), and {{w|tool steel}} (high-speed, hot-work, and cold-work grades). The company also produces {{w|billet|steel billets}} for sale to downstream manufacturers.

The company is listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor and has a market capitalisation of approximately $2.0 billion. The Masdeu family, descendants of founder Pere Masdeu i Font, retains a 20 per cent stake. The FIDB holds an 8 per cent stake and the BPF holds a 5 per cent stake. The company employs approximately 2,500 people.

== Raw materials and energy ==

Balboa's steel industry is characterised by a fundamental asymmetry: the country has abundant, low-cost energy but lacks domestic reserves of the two key raw materials required for primary steelmaking — {{w|iron ore}} and {{w|coking coal}}.

=== Iron ore ===

All of Balboa's iron ore is imported. During the Zong Balboa period, iron ore was sourced primarily from [[Louyang]], then a Zong dependency with substantial deposits. Since reunification, however, the supply has shifted to BTPMEU partners: [[Tapuya]] now supplies approximately 80 per cent of Balboa's iron ore imports, with the remainder sourced from [[Lacashe]]. Iron ore is shipped to the bulk cargo terminals at the [[Port de Portnou]] and transported by rail to the ACI steelworks at [[Les Drassanes]]. Only ACI's blast furnaces require iron ore; FBL and GMS operate EAFs that use scrap metal rather than iron ore.

=== Coking coal ===

{{w|Coking coal}} is imported from [[Tapuya]] and [[Lacashe]]. As with iron ore, only ACI's blast furnaces require coking coal. The EAF producers (FBL and GMS) do not use coking coal, which gives them a significant cost advantage in the Balboan context — they are not exposed to coking coal price volatility or supply disruptions.

=== Scrap metal ===

Domestic {{w|scrap metal}} collection supplies the EAF producers (FBL and GMS) with approximately 60 per cent of their input requirements. The remaining 40 per cent is imported, primarily from [[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]] within the BTPMEU. The Balboan government has invested in scrap metal collection infrastructure, including recycling centres and scrap processing facilities, to support the EAF steel industry and reduce dependence on imported raw materials.

=== Energy ===

Balboa's abundant {{w|hydroelectricity|hydropower}} is the steel industry's principal competitive advantage. The EAF route is highly electricity-intensive, and Balboa's low hydropower costs — among the cheapest in Adonia — make EAF steelmaking significantly cheaper than in countries that rely on fossil fuel power generation. This advantage is amplified by the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] on manufactured exports, which effectively subsidises the cost of electricity for export-oriented producers.

The Sant Bart plants (FBL and GMS) are supplied by the Yalabirga hydropower system, while the Les Drassanes plant (ACI) draws power from the national grid, which is predominantly hydroelectric. ACI's blast furnaces also use {{w|metallurgical coke}} as a fuel and reducing agent, which is produced from imported coking coal at the Les Drassanes coking plant.

=== Graphite electrodes ===

The EAF producers (FBL, GMS, and ACI's EAF operations) rely on {{w|graphite electrode}}s, which are supplied domestically by [[Shoko Denko Balboa]] (SD Balboa), the specialty chemicals subsidiary of the Sanese conglomerate [[Shoko Denko]]. SD Balboa produces synthetic graphite from {{w|petroleum coke}} — a byproduct of [[CIEA]]'s [[Badia Curta]] refinery — at its Badia Curta plant. This creates a vertical link between the petroleum and steel industries: CIEA's refinery produces petroleum coke, SD Balboa processes it into graphite electrodes, and the EAF steelmakers use the electrodes to produce steel.

== Products and markets ==

=== Products ===

The Balboan steel industry produces three main categories of products:

* '''Long products''': Reinforcing bar (rebar), wire rod, merchant bar, and structural sections (I-beams, H-beams, angles). These are produced by all three companies and are primarily used in the construction and infrastructure sectors. Long products account for approximately 65 per cent of total production by volume.
* '''Flat products''': Hot-rolled coil, cold-rolled coil, and plate. These are produced exclusively by ACI at its Les Drassanes works and are used in the manufacturing, automotive, and shipbuilding sectors. Flat products account for approximately 25 per cent of total production by volume.
* '''Specialty steel''': Stainless steel, alloy steel, and tool steel. These are produced exclusively by GMS and are used in the manufacturing, tooling, and semiconductor equipment supply chains. Specialty steel accounts for approximately 10 per cent of total production by volume.

=== Markets ===

The domestic market absorbs approximately 70 per cent of Balboan steel production. The largest domestic customers are the construction sector ([[Grup Ciutat Nova]], [[Construccions de Sant Cristòfor]], [[Grup JJ]], [[Cases Justes]]) and the manufacturing sector ([[Companyia Grand Balboa]]). The infrastructure sector — including the [[Canal of Balboa|Balboa Canal]], railways, and port facilities — is also a significant customer.

Exports account for approximately 30 per cent of production. The primary export markets are the BTPMEU partners ([[Potocsí]] and [[Tapuya]]), which absorb the majority of exports under the [[negative tariff]] regime. Smaller volumes are exported to [[Balisca]] and [[Lacashe]]. The export of flat products to Okaiken and Balisca for the automotive and semiconductor supply chains is a growing but still modest segment.

